WEBVTT

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.253
You have a finished embroidery file, bought or given to you, and want to improve it?

00:00:10.253 --> 00:00:15.393
The first step: open it and look closely at how it is stitched.

00:00:15.393 --> 00:00:17.840
That is what this video shows.

00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:25.971
Simply drag the file into the Files field on the left.

00:00:25.971 --> 00:00:33.008
That works with PES, DST and the other common formats.

00:00:33.008 --> 00:00:37.048
Your file stays on your device.

00:00:37.048 --> 00:00:43.173
Without a file of your own, try “Load example”.

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:48.206
The file even tells you which hoop it was made for.

00:00:48.206 --> 00:00:53.227
One click on the suggestion, and the hoop sits around the design.

00:00:54.333 --> 00:00:56.624
The screen has three columns.

00:00:56.624 --> 00:01:03.024
On the left is everything about the file: hoop, saving and how the stitches look.

00:01:03.024 --> 00:01:06.579
In the middle your design lies on the canvas.

00:01:06.579 --> 00:01:13.373
On the right are the colours in the order they are stitched, and below them the jumps.

00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:17.129
Zoom in with the mouse wheel.

00:01:17.129 --> 00:01:24.474
Point at a stitch and it says which number it is, what stitch type and how long.

00:01:24.474 --> 00:01:29.707
Dragging moves the picture, “Fit” shows everything again.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:39.450
And now the best part: with the space bar the machine stitches before your eyes, colour by colour.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:44.040
First cream, then orange, gold and the red knitting.

00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:51.438
At the bottom you see which stitch you are at and how long the machine takes to get here.

00:01:51.438 --> 00:01:53.988
Just drag the slider back and forth.

00:01:53.988 --> 00:01:57.813
The buttons on the outside jump from colour to colour.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:08.228
Point at a colour on the right and the rest steps back, so you see exactly what is stitched in dark brown.

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:12.980
The eye hides a colour, and “Show all” brings it back.

00:02:14.067 --> 00:02:19.526
With realistic threads you see beforehand how the cat looks on the fabric.

00:02:19.526 --> 00:02:24.027
That way you know your file before you change anything in it.
